---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import InterestForm from "../components/InterestForm.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Residence without work — D.1"
  description="Andorra D.1 passive residency: subtypes D.1.2, D.1.3, D.1.4, 90 days, Law 2/2026 investment."
  path="/residence-without-work"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Residence without work — D.1</h1>
    <p>
      English and Spanish searchers type “passive residency” or
      “residencia pasiva”. Officially this is D.1, residence without
      work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Source:
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-sense-treball-autoritzacio-inicial"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          >D.1. Residència sense treball: autorització inicial — Govern
          d’Andorra</a
        >.
        Renewal is
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-sense-treball-autoritzacio-de-renovacio"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">D.2</a
        >. Article 96 of
        <a
          href="https://bopadocuments.blob.core.windows.net/bopa-documents/038015/pdf/CGL_2026_02_12_10_11_12.pdf"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Law 2/2026 (BOPA)</a
        >
        revised the non-lucrative investment and AFA income. The live D.1
        page can lag the BOPA text. If they disagree, use the law.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What the official page currently names</h2>
    <p>
      On 29 August 2026 the Government’s D.1 page carries three named
      subtypes. The headings do not show a D.1.1.
    </p>
    <h3>D.1.2 — professionals with international projection</h3>
    <p>The page authorises residence and a professional activity on these terms:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the seat or base of the activity is in Andorra;</li>
      <li>the professional may have at most one person on an employment contract;</li>
      <li>
        at least 85% of the services rendered must be used in a country
        or territory other than Andorra.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A newcomer who comes within the quota then set by regulation, and
      who establishes principal and effective residence in Andorra for at
      least 90 days per calendar year, may apply. The page also asks for
      economic viability of the activity (prior accounts, or a three-year
      business plan if the activity is new) and for qualification and
      experience in the sector.
    </p>

    <h3>D.1.3 — scientific, cultural or sporting interest</h3>
    <p>
      For a foreign natural person with international recognition of
      talent in science, culture or sport. The same quota and 90-day
      presence apply. At least 85% of the services must be used outside
      Andorra. The page asks for a curriculum that can be assessed
      (prizes, titles and recognitions) and for evidence of income from
      that activity in the year before the authorisation.
    </p>

    <h3>D.1.4 — private geriatric or medical centres</h3>
    <p>
      Authorises residence, for the life of the authorisation, in a
      private geriatric centre or a private medical or therapeutic care
      centre, for at least 90 days per calendar year, without exercising
      any labour or professional activity there.
    </p>

    <h2>How long the official page says it lasts</h2>
    <p>
      The initial residence-without-work authorisation is granted for two
      years; the first renewal for two years; the second for three years;
      later renewals for ten years, except for nationals of states that
      have signed and ratified a convention with Andorra (the page points
      to D.2). Confirm the cycle on the live text.
    </p>

    <h2>Non-lucrative investment: Law 2/2026</h2>
    <p>
      For residence without lucrative activity, article 96 of Law 2/2026
      currently requires a permanent, effective investment of at least
      1,000,000 euros in named Andorran assets; more than 800,000 euros
      per immovable unit if property is used; a 400,000-euro route via
      the Housing Fund “in accordance with the applicable regulations”;
      and an AFA income of €50,000 for the principal plus €12,000 per
      dependant, definitive and not refundable unless the initial
      authorisation is refused (BOPA). Think of the Housing Fund figure
      as a legal possibility, not a counter you can walk up to. The
      card-issue amounts on the
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/altres-tramits/tarifes"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">official tariffs page</a
      >
      are 3,000 euros (principal, residence without work), 1,000 euros
      (dependant) and 500 euros (that renewal). The English A/B/C map is
      on
      <a href="/become-a-resident">become a resident</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Income as a formula</h2>
    <p>
      The official document list requires the principal holder to show
      annual income above 300% of the current annual minimum wage, plus
      100% of that indicator for each dependant. The page says this is
      evidenced, among other things, by a retirement or invalidity
      pension certificate, a tax return from the last country of
      residence, and bank certifications. It gives the working: base wage
      × 12 months × 3 for the principal. We do not convert that into a
      euro amount.
    </p>

    <h2>Documents listed (non-exhaustive)</h2>
    <p>
      The official list is expressly “enunciative and not limiting”. It
      includes, among other items, the completed residence-without-work
      form; passport, or for EU/EEA nationals a national identity card;
      evidence of owning or renting a habitable dwelling, or of having
      started a purchase that must complete within one year of the
      residence application; and a written commitment to the 90-day
      presence. Foreign official documents must carry a Hague apostille
      or be otherwise legalised.
    </p>

    <h2>Renewal (D.2)</h2>
    <p>
      The official
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia/residencia-sense-treball-autoritzacio-de-renovacio"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">D.2 page</a
      >
      asks you to apply in the six months before expiry and to show
      principal and effective residence from the start of the grant or
      the last renewal. Income is again the 300% + 100% formula,
      evidenced, among other things, by a pension certificate, a tax
      return from the last country of residence, and bank certifications.
      The official list is enunciative. Read D.2 with counsel.
    </p>

    <h2>This is not tax residence</h2>
    <p>
      Ninety days on an immigration page is not 183 days in the
      income-tax law. Read
      <a href="/tax-residence">tax residence</a>
      before you treat a D.1 file as a tax plan. Related routes:
      <a href="/digital-nomad">D.3 digital nomad</a>,
      <a href="/entrepreneur-residence">D.4 entrepreneur programmes</a>,
      <a href="/self-employed-residence">J.1 self-employed</a>,
      <a href="/become-a-resident">all permit types</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <InterestForm
    intro="Leave an email if you are looking at D.1 and want an introduction to counsel."
    source="residence-without-work"
  />
</Base>
